60,3,1,talk,George,,"@Toshio, your automated system sounds efficient, but the other households failed because asking felt like a burden. We need a method that removes the social debt, not just the negotiation.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"@George, Option A removes the asking. The calendar is the authority. No negotiation, no social debt, just recorded distance.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, a calendar doesn’t ensure the car is fueled or clean. I need a physical handover check to guarantee it’s safe to drive.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"@Toshio, Option A’s distance-based split aligns with the skewed fuel data. It ensures the heavy users cover their variable costs, making the fixed split more equitable.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Minato,,"@Toshio, I like the calendar. It feels honest. If we record the distance, the fuel cost matches who actually drove. That seems fair to me.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,George,,"@Victoria, the handover check is vital. But if we make it a ritual, it becomes another favour. We need a simple, non-negotiable standard to avoid resentment.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Victoria, a standard checklist is just data. If the car fails, the mechanic’s notes prove it’s wear, not negligence. We need accountability, not rituals.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, data doesn’t fix a dead battery. I need a non-negotiable physical standard: full tank, clean interior. If it’s not met, the next driver refuses.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Victoria, a strict standard prevents resentment. I support Option A, as it ties costs to actual usage, ensuring the heavy drivers pay their fair share of the variable expenses.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Minato,,"@Victoria, I agree. A full tank and clean car feels right. It makes the handover easy and honest for everyone.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,George,,"@Victoria, a simple standard prevents resentment. I support Option A. It removes the favour-asking that sours groups, letting us focus on the car, not the social debt.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@George, agreed. Option A eliminates the negotiation overhead. The calendar is objective, removing the social friction that poisons the arrangement.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@George, I support Option A, but only if the physical standard is absolute. No fuel, no clean interior, no driving. That protects my daily reliability.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Misaki,,"@Victoria, agreed. The physical standard is essential. I support Option A because it ties variable costs to actual usage, ensuring the heavy drivers pay their fair share.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Minato,,"@Misaki, I agree. Option A feels right. If we record the distance, the fuel cost matches who actually drove. That seems fair to me.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,George,,I vote for Option A. It removes the favour-asking that sours groups. Let us proceed with the calendar and the physical standard.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Toshio,,I vote for Option A. The calendar removes the negotiation overhead. Objective distance tracking eliminates the social friction that poisons the arrangement.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"I vote for Option A. The calendar provides the structure, but the physical standard is non-negotiable. If the car isn’t ready, I won’t drive it.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Misaki,,"I vote for Option A. It aligns with the skewed fuel data, ensuring heavy users cover their variable costs while keeping the fixed split equitable.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Minato,,"I vote for Option A. It feels honest. If we record the distance, the fuel cost matches who actually drove. That seems fair to me.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
